.TH smi-spring-boot "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-spring-boot \- Start a Spring Boot executable jar, web/API application or CLI tool, with an optional administrator configuration file and the log location
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-spring-boot
.RI [ ARGUMENT ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-spring-boot
starts the Spring Boot executable jar of \fBMICROSERVICE_JAR_FILE_PATH\fR with \fBjava\fR(1) of \fBPATH\fR and the Spring Boot
launcher \fBorg.springframework.boot.loader.launch.JarLauncher\fR, and sets Spring Boot properties from the
environment variables below. Every \fIARGUMENT\fR goes to the application unchanged, spaces and empty arguments
included: options such as \fB\-\-server.port=8082\fR for a web/API application, or the arguments of a CLI tool.
.PP
An environment variable that is unset or empty adds nothing, so the value inside the jar stays in force. The
values are taken as given: the caller, for example the systemd unit, sets them right.
.PP
The shell is replaced by \fBjava\fR (exec): the signals of a service manager, for example SIGTERM of
\fBsystemctl stop\fR, reach the JVM, and the exit status of the command is the exit status of the application,
143 after SIGTERM.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
\fBMICROSERVICE_JAR_FILE_PATH\fR
The executable Spring Boot jar.
.TP
\fBMICROSERVICE_PROFILES\fR
Sets \fBspring.profiles.active\fR, several profiles separated by commas.
.TP
\fBMICROSERVICE_OPTIONAL_CONFIG_FILE_PATH\fR
Sets \fBspring.config.additional-location\fR, marked \fBoptional:\fR: a file that does not exist is skipped
without an error. The file is the place for an
administrator to change values without a new build: it is read after the configuration inside the jar, the
profile specific files included, so every value written in it wins over the jar. Environment variables and command
line arguments still win over the file.
.TP
\fBMICROSERVICE_LOG_DIR_PATH\fR
Sets \fBlogging.file.path\fR, the directory of the log files.
.TP
\fBMICROSERVICE_LOG_FILE_NAME\fR
Sets \fBlogging.file.name\fR, the name of the log file inside \fBMICROSERVICE_LOG_DIR_PATH\fR when that is set:
Spring Boot itself would ignore the directory when the name is set.
.TP
\fBMICROSERVICE_LOG_CONFIG_FILE_PATH\fR
Sets \fBlogging.config\fR, a logging configuration file (for example logback.xml) outside the jar, for an
administrator to change levels, appenders or patterns. Set it only when the file is there.
.TP
\fBJDK_JAVA_OPTIONS\fR
Options of the JVM, for example memory settings. Read by \fBjava\fR itself, not by this command.
.SH LOGGING CONFIGURATION OF THE APPLICATION
.PP
Spring Boot passes \fBlogging.file.path\fR and \fBlogging.file.name\fR to the logging system as \fBLOG_PATH\fR
and \fBLOG_FILE\fR. The default configuration of Spring Boot uses them. An application with a logback.xml of its
own uses them only when it refers to them with defaults instead of setting them, for example
\fB<property name="LOG_PATH" value="${LOG_PATH:-.}"/>\fR and \fB<file>${LOG_FILE}</file>\fR; a fixed value in the
file wins over the environment variables.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
The exit status of the application, 0 when it ended successfully.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
A systemd unit of a web/API application:
.PP
.nf
.RS
Environment=PATH=/opt/jdk/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
Environment=MICROSERVICE_JAR_FILE_PATH=/opt/setmy.info/lib/example.jar
Environment=MICROSERVICE_PROFILES=live
Environment=MICROSERVICE_OPTIONAL_CONFIG_FILE_PATH=/opt/setmy.info/etc/example/example.yaml
Environment=MICROSERVICE_LOG_DIR_PATH=/var/opt/setmy.info/microservice
Environment=MICROSERVICE_LOG_FILE_NAME=example.log
ExecStart=/opt/setmy.info/bin/smi-spring-boot
SuccessExitStatus=143
.RE
.fi
.PP
A CLI tool with its arguments:
.PP
.nf
.RS
MICROSERVICE_JAR_FILE_PATH=/opt/setmy.info/lib/tool.jar smi-spring-boot import --dry-run "file name.csv"
.RE
.fi
